  "notes": "West leads the ♠6 against 3NT. West's lead gives South a 3 way choice as to which spade should be played from dummy. It seems natural to play low from dummy at trick 1.\n\nHowever, if East is able to win the ♠K, he will return a heart. When South wins the ♥A, he will have to plays clubs without loss. The percentage play is to finesse West for the ♣K. If West has all 3 clubs, however, South lacks the hand entries to finesse again in clubs.\n\nThe way to guard against the above distribution is to play the ♠Q from dummy at trick 1. If East wins the ♠K and switches to hearts, the ♥A and ♠J can be used as hand entries to finesse in clubs. If the ♠Q holds, South can afford to give up a trick to the ♣K. South will take at least 9 tricks (2 spades, the ♥A, and 6 clubs).\n\nPlaying the ♠A from dummy at trick 1 and continuing with the ♠Q is not good enough. East will duck, denying South a second hand entry. The defense might later take 2 spade tricks, 2 diamond tricks, and the ♣K.",
